# Project: Supply Chain Order Assistant

A portfolio project: an AI agent that helps a manufacturing company's Customer Service team
answer questions about orders, inventory and shipments, and propose (never apply) order changes
that a human must approve. It must look like real, production-minded .NET work.

## Architecture (do not deviate without asking me)
- `src/Erp.Api` — mock ERP: ASP.NET Core Web API, EF Core + SQLite, Swagger.
- `src/Agent.Tools` — class library: agent tools (C# methods) that call Erp.Api ONLY via HttpClient,
  never the database directly. Shared by the console app, Chat.Api and Mcp.Server.
- `src/Agent.Console` — console app to chat with the agent (week 1).
- `src/Chat.Api` — ASP.NET Core API exposing POST /chat (week 3).
- `src/Mcp.Server` — MCP server exposing the same tools (week 2).
- `web/` — Next.js + TypeScript chat UI and approvals page (week 3).
- `tests/` — xUnit unit and integration tests; `evals/` — eval set and runner.
- `docs/policies/` — Markdown policy documents used for RAG.

## Tech rules
- Latest stable .NET and C#. Nullable enabled. Async all the way. Use records for DTOs.
- Talk to the LLM through `Microsoft.Extensions.AI` (`IChatClient`, `IEmbeddingGenerator`) so the
  provider (Azure AI Foundry / Azure OpenAI, GitHub Models, OpenAI) is a config change only.
- Agent: Microsoft Agent Framework (`Microsoft.Agents.AI`). If an API is unclear, check the
  official docs (Microsoft Learn / github.com/microsoft/agent-framework) instead of guessing.
  If the package is unusable, tell me and propose Semantic Kernel as fallback.
- Secrets: never in code or appsettings.json. Use `dotnet user-secrets` locally and environment
  variables otherwise. Config keys: `AI:Provider`, `AI:Endpoint`, `AI:ApiKey`, `AI:ChatModel`,
  `AI:EmbeddingModel`, `ErpApi:BaseUrl`.
- The agent has NO ability to approve or reject change requests. Only humans (Supervisor role) can.
- Every tool call is logged with structured logging (ILogger): tool name, arguments, duration ms,
  success/failure.
- Tool descriptions (`[Description]`) must be precise: what it does, when to use it, what it returns.

## How to work with me
- Before a large change, give a short plan (files to create/modify) and wait for my OK.
- Keep changes small and focused on the current step. Don't build future steps early.
- After code changes: run `dotnet build` and `dotnet test` and fix errors before saying "done".
- Explain non-obvious decisions in 1–3 sentences; I want to be able to explain them in an interview.
- Suggest a concise conventional commit message at the end of each step.
